{ Endgames from Capablanca, world champion from 1921, and Edward Lasker, a leading master of the 1910s. The spare pawn step: a pawn that has not moved can go one square or two, so you choose which side must move when the pawns meet. [%csl Gg7] }
1. Kh1 { His king steps into the corner, the only way to keep his pawn on h2 guarded. You are Black and a pawn up: win it. Push your g pawn two squares. [%cal Yg7g5,Rh1h2] } 1... g5 { Two squares at once. Count it: two more steps take your pawn to g3, and his king, going to g1 and back, is in the corner when it lands. [%cal Gg5g3] } (1... g6 { One square only. Count it now: three more steps to g3, and his king is on g1, not in the corner, when your pawn lands. [%cal Rg7g6] } 2. Kg1 g5 3. Kh1 g4 4. Kg1 g3 5. hxg3 hxg3 6. Kh1 g2+ 7. Kg1 Kg3 { Stalemate: a draw. His king on g1 has no move and is not in check; the single step spoiled your count. [%csl Rg1] }) (1... Kg4 { The king goes hunting on g4, but it stops covering g2, and his king can escape the corner. [%csl Rg2] } 2. Kg2 g5 3. h3+ Kf4 4. Kf2 { A draw: his king on f2 and his pawn on h3 hold both your pawns, and neither gets through. [%csl Rf2] }) 2. Kg1 { His only move, because your king on h3 covers g2. Keep the count going: push the pawn again. [%cal Yg5g4,Gh3g2] } 2... g4 { Your king stays on h3 and watches his pawn: if his king wanders off, your king takes h2. [%csl Rh2] } (2... Kg4 { Your king steps off h3 in the middle of the march, and the door to g2 opens for his king. [%csl Rg2] } 3. Kg2 h3+ 4. Kh1 { A draw: your h pawn is stuck in front of his pawn on h2, and his king is back beside it. [%csl Rh3] }) 3. Kh1 { Back into the corner to guard h2. Your pawn has only one move now, and it is the right one: on to g3. [%cal Rh1h2] } 3... g3 { The pawn lands on g3 while his king sits in the corner: that is what the jump at the start bought you. [%csl Gg3] } 4. hxg3 { He takes your pawn. Take back with the h pawn, so your king stays right beside the new pawn. [%cal Yh4g3] } 4... hxg3 { Your king stays beside the new g pawn, and his king has one square left, g1: it has to step out of the corner. [%csl Gg1] } (4... Kxg3 { The king takes, and you are left with a lone h pawn against his king in the corner. [%csl Rh1] } 5. Kg1 h3 6. Kh1 h2 { Stalemate: his king on h1 has no move, so it is a draw. A rook pawn cannot beat a king sitting in its corner. [%csl Rh1] }) (4... Kg4 { Your king heads for his pawn on g3, but that pawn attacks your h pawn, and a trade leaves nothing. [%cal Rg3h4] } 5. gxh4 Kxh4 { Bare kings: the pawns are gone, and nobody can win. A draw. }) 5. Kg1 { Forced: g1 was his only square. Push on to g2. [%cal Yg3g2] } 5... g2 { Pawn to g2. His king has one square left, f2, and it must go there. [%csl Rf2] } (5... Kg4 { Your king leaves h3, so g2 is free, and his king can step in front of your pawn. [%csl Rg2] } 6. Kg2 Kf4 7. Kg1 Kf3 8. Kf1 g2+ 9. Kg1 Kg3 { Stalemate: his king on g1 has no move, a draw. Once his king stood in front of your pawn, there was no way through. [%csl Rg1] }) (5... Kh4 { Your king steps away to h4, and his king can take the square in front of your pawn. [%csl Rg2] } 6. Kg2 { A draw: his king blocks your pawn, and your king cannot drive it off without giving stalemate. [%csl Rg3] }) 6. Kf2 { His king now stands right beside the queening square. Queen now and he takes her, so guard the pawn first: king to h2. [%cal Yh3h2][%csl Rg1] } 6... Kh2 { Your king guards the pawn and the square in front of it, and his king cannot touch either. Watch the queen arrive. [%tail][%cal Gg2g1] } (6... g1=Q+ { A new queen at once, with check. But look where his king stands. [%csl Rf2] } 7. Kxg1 { His king takes her on g1: bare kings, and a draw. Your king had to guard the queening square first. [%csl Rg1] }) (6... Kg4 { Your king walks away, and nothing guards the pawn on g2. [%csl Rg2] } 7. Kxg2 { His king eats the pawn: bare kings, a draw. [%csl Rg2] }) (6... Kh4 { The king steps back to h4, and the pawn on g2 is left alone. [%csl Rg2] } 7. Kxg2 { His king takes your last pawn, and bare kings are a draw. }) 7. Kf3 g1=Q { A new queen on g1. The jump at the start made his king step out of the corner at the end: that is the spare pawn step. [%csl Gg1] } 0-1


