{ His king is on g1 this time, and it is your move. You are Black and a pawn up: win. Count the moves before you touch your pawn. }
1... g6 { One square, and the count works: that is the spare pawn step. Your pawn reaches g3 while his king is in the corner, so he has to step out. Watch. [%tail] } (1... g5 { Two squares at once. Count it: two more steps to g3, and by then his king has stepped to g1. [%cal Rg7g5] } 2. Kh1 g4 3. Kg1 g3 4. hxg3 hxg3 5. Kh1 g2+ 6. Kg1 Kg3 { Stalemate, a draw: his king on g1 has no square left. The jump arrived one move too early. [%csl Rg1] }) (1... Kg4 { Your king leaves h3, and his king can walk out through g2. [%csl Rg2] } 2. Kg2 g5 3. h3+ Kf4 4. Kf2 { A draw: his king reached f2, and with his pawn on h3 it holds both of yours. [%csl Rf2] }) 2. Kh1 g5 3. Kg1 g4 4. Kh1 g3 5. hxg3 hxg3 6. Kg1 g2 7. Kf2 Kh2 8. Kf3 g1=Q { A new queen on g1. One step instead of two put his king in the corner at the right moment. [%csl Gg1] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca and Lasker endgames: the distant passed pawn, make one far away"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/Ag1kMK5z"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Diagram 59, line 4052"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[FEN "8/8/1pp2k1p/6p1/1PP3P1/P4K2/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/1pp2k1p/6p1/1PP3P1/P4K2/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ You have White and play to win. The distant passed pawn, in an example by Edward Lasker, a leading master of the 1910s: a free pawn far from the kings, which drags his king away. Make one: push the a pawn. [%cal Ya3a4] }
1. a4 { The a pawn heads for a5 to swap itself for his b pawn. Three of your pawns face two of his on the queen side, so one of yours can come out free. } (1. b5 { The b pawn charges past instead, and he swaps it off with his c pawn. } 1... cxb5 2. cxb5 Ke5 3. Ke3 Kd5 4. a4 Kc5 { A draw: his king on c5 sits right beside your pawns, so a pawn that gets free there is caught at once. It is not far from the kings at all. [%csl Rc5] }) (1. c5 { The c pawn charges at his b pawn instead. } 1... bxc5 2. bxc5 Ke5 3. Ke3 Kd5 4. Kd3 Kxc5 { A draw: you do get a free a pawn, but it stands only two steps from his king on c5, which has just eaten your c pawn. [%csl Rc5] }) (1. Ke4 { The king steps forward first, and the a pawn waits. } 1... b5 2. c5 { A draw: his pawn got to b5 first and the queen side is blocked. Nobody can make progress there now. [%csl Rb5] }) 1... Ke6 { His king steps toward the queen side. Push on to a5: if he takes, your b pawn takes back and walks free. [%cal Ya4a5] } 2. a5 { Your pawn on a5 hits b6. If he takes, your b pawn takes back and becomes a free pawn on the a column. } (2. b5 { The b pawn goes past instead, and the queen side is locked. } 2... Kd6 3. Ke4 Kc5 { A draw: with your b pawn gone past, nothing can take back on a5, so the swap that makes a far free pawn is gone. [%csl Ga5] }) (2. c5 { The c pawn charges at b6 instead. } 2... bxc5 3. a5 cxb4 4. a6 b3 5. a7 b2 6. a8=Q b1=Q 7. Qxc6+ Ke5 8. Qc7+ { A draw by endless checks: both pawns queened, and your queen took his c pawn and keeps checking his king. [%csl Gc7] }) (2. Ke4 { The king steps forward to e4 before the pawn moves. } 2... Kd7 { Also wins: the king went first, and the pawn can still go to a5 next move. [%cal Ga4a5] }) (2. Ke3 { The king steps to e3 first. } 2... Kd6 3. a5 bxa5 4. bxa5 { Also wins: the king took a step toward the middle, and the a pawn still came out free a move later. [%csl Ga5] }) (2. Kf2 { The king steps back to f2 instead. } 2... Kd6 { Still a win, but the hard way: your king stepped back while his heads for your pawns, and from here every step must be exact. [%cal Ga4a5] }) (2. Ke2 { The king backs away to e2. } 2... Kd6 { Still a win, but only just: your king left the middle and must race over to the queen side with no time to spare. [%cal Ga4a5] }) (2. Kg3 { The king moves over to g3. } 2... Kd6 { A win still, but g3 points your king the wrong way, and the race to the queen side allows no slip at all. [%cal Ga4a5] }) (2. Kg2 { The king walks back to g2. } 2... Kd6 { Still winning, but your king is now far from everything. Only an exact race to the queen side keeps the win. [%cal Ga4a5] }) 2... bxa5 { He takes on a5. Take back with the b pawn. } 3. bxa5 { Your pawn on a5 is free and far from both kings: a distant passed pawn. His king must go and catch it. [%csl Ga5] } (3. b5 { The b pawn pushes past instead of taking back. } 3... cxb5 4. cxb5 Kd5 5. Ke2 a4 { Lost: now he has the free pawn far from the kings, on a4, and your king must run after it. [%csl Ra4] }) (3. c5 { The c pawn steps forward and leaves your b pawn to him. } 3... axb4 4. Ke2 Kd5 5. Ke3 Kxc5 6. Ke4 b3 { Lost: his king has eaten your c pawn, and his free b pawn is on b3 while your king stands on the other wing. [%csl Rb3] }) 3... Kd6 { His king heads off after your a pawn. Send yours the other way, toward his king side pawns: king to e4. [%cal Yf3e4] } 4. Ke4 { Your king heads for g5 and h6 while his king has to deal with your a pawn. Now watch. [%cal Ge4f5][%tail] } (4. c5+ { A check with the c pawn! But it walks straight into his king. } 4... Kxc5 5. Ke4 Kb5 6. Kf5 Kxa5 { Lost: his king ate both your queen side pawns, and now the free pawn is his c pawn. [%csl Rc6] }) (4. a6 { The a pawn runs on at once. } 4... Kc7 5. Ke4 Kb6 6. Kf5 Kxa6 { Also wins: his king had to go all the way to a6 to catch the pawn, and yours stands next to his king side pawns. [%csl Ga6] }) (4. Ke3 { The king steps to e3, a little off the straight road. } 4... Kc7 { Also wins, just a step slower: from e3 your king still reaches his king side pawns in time. [%cal Ge3f4] }) (4. Kf2 { The king backs away to f2. } 4... Kc7 { Still a win, but now your king has to race to the queen side instead, and every step of that race is forced. [%cal Gf3e4] }) (4. Ke2 { The king steps back to e2. } 4... Kc7 { Still winning, but only by switching plans: your king must go after his queen side pawns, with no move to waste. [%cal Gf3e4] }) (4. Kg3 { The king slides over to g3. } 4... Kc7 { Still a win, but g3 is a detour: your king must rush to the other wing now, and one slip throws it away. [%cal Gf3e4] }) (4. Kg2 { The king heads back to g2. } 4... Kc7 { A win still, but a hard one: from g2 your king must race to the queen side, and each step there is the only one. [%cal Gf3e4] }) 4... Kc7 5. Kf5 Kb7 6. Kg6 Ka6 7. Kxh6 Kxa5 8. Kxg5 Kb4 9. Kf5 Kxc4 10. g5 Kb3 11. g6 c5 12. g7 c4 13. g8=Q { A new queen on g8: the distant passed pawn dragged his king away from his king side pawns. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


