{ You have Black and play to win. Each side has one free pawn on the king side, yours on the h column and his on the f column, and the queen side pawns face each other. }
6. Kh4 { His king goes after your pawn on h5. Every move loses for him here, and this is his most stubborn try. How do you win? } 6... Kf5 { Your king steps forward to attack his f pawn. If he takes your h pawn, you take his, and your king finishes nearer the queen side. } (6... Kg6 { Your king steps to g6 to guard the h pawn. } 7. f5+ Kxf5 8. Kxh5 { Also wins: the free pawns come off all the same, and your king on f5 is two columns nearer the queen side than his. [%csl Gf5] }) (6... a5 { Your a pawn steps to a5, and his b pawn simply takes it. } 7. bxa5 { Lost: his pawn on a5 is free now, and your king is far too far away to catch it. [%csl Ra5] }) (6... Ke6 { Your king heads for the queen side at once. } 7. Kg5 Kd5 8. Kxh5 Kc4 9. f5 Kb3 10. f6 { Lost: he took your h pawn as well, and his f pawn is two steps from queening while your king is still busy with his queen side pawns. [%csl Rf6] }) (6... Ke7 { Your king backs away to e7. } 7. Kxh5 Ke6 8. Kg6 { Lost: he took your h pawn for nothing, and now his king escorts the only free pawn left, on the f column. [%cal Rf4f8] }) (6... Kf7 { Your king steps back to f7. } 7. Kxh5 Ke6 8. Kg6 { Lost: stepping back gave him your h pawn for free, and his f pawn is the one free pawn on the board. [%csl Rf4] }) (6... Kg7 { Your king retreats to g7. } 7. Kxh5 Kf6 8. Kg4 { Lost: your h pawn is gone for nothing, and his king stands guard beside his f pawn. [%csl Rf4] }) 7. Kxh5 { He takes your h pawn. } 7... Kxf4 { You take his f pawn. Both free pawns are gone, and your king on f4 is two columns nearer the queen side than his. [%csl Gf4] } (7... Ke4 { Your king runs for the queen side and leaves his f pawn alone. } 8. Kg4 Kd3 9. f5 Kc2 10. f6 { Lost: his f pawn stands on f6 with nothing in front of it, and your king is busy far away. [%csl Rf6] }) (7... Ke6 { Your king steps back to e6. } 8. Kg4 Kd5 9. f5 { Lost: his king now guards the f pawn, and your king on d5 cannot stop it and win his pawns too. [%csl Rf5] }) (7... Kf6 { Your king steps to f6, in front of his pawn. } 8. Kg4 Ke6 9. f5+ { Lost: his king and pawn come forward together, and your king has to give way. [%csl Rf5] }) (7... a5 { Your a pawn goes to a5, where his b pawn can take it for free. } 8. bxa5 Ke4 9. a6 Kf5 10. a7 Kxf4 11. a8=Q { Lost: his a pawn ran all the way to a8 and queened while your king ate his f pawn. [%csl Ra8] }) 8. Kg6 { His king turns back toward the queen side. } 8... Ke5 { Your king heads the same way, and it is two columns ahead. Now watch the race. [%cal Ge5d4][%tail] } (8... Ke4 { Your king takes the road through e4. } 9. Kg5 Kd3 { Also wins: e4 is just as short a road to his queen side pawns. [%cal Gd3c2] }) (8... Ke3 { Your king steps to e3. } 9. Kf5 Kd2 { Also wins: your king heads for his pawns as fast as by e5. [%csl Ga3,Gb4] }) (8... Kf3 { Your king steps to f3. } 9. Kf5 Ke2 { Also wins, one move slower: f3 is a step off the shortest road, and you still arrive first. [%csl Ga3] }) (8... Kg4 { Your king steps to g4, away from the queen side. } 9. Kf6 Kf3 10. Ke5 { Lost: now his king is the one nearer the queen side pawns, and it gets there first. [%csl Ra6,Rb5] }) (8... Kg3 { Your king steps to g3. } 9. Kf5 Kf2 10. Ke4 { Lost: your king walked away from the race, and his king reaches your queen side pawns first. [%csl Ra6,Rb5] }) (8... a5 { Your a pawn moves to a5, straight into his b pawn's reach. } 9. bxa5 Ke3 10. a6 { Lost: his b pawn took on a5 and ran on to a6, too far ahead for your king to catch. [%csl Ra6] }) 9. Kg5 Kd4 10. Kf4 Kc3 11. Ke3 Kb2 12. Kd2 Kxa3 13. Kc3 Ka4 14. Kb2 Kxb4 { Both his queen side pawns are gone. Swapping the free pawns left your king ahead: the distant passed pawn at work. [%csl Ga6,Gb5] } 0-1


[Event "Capablanca and Lasker endgames: zugzwang, hand him the move"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/PQndUNBX"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Source "Edward Lasker, Chess Strategy (1915); Diagram 73, line 4647"]
[IntendedResult "win"]
[FEN "8/4k2p/2P1n2P/3KB3/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/4k2p/2P1n2P/3KB3/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]

{ You have White and play to win. Zugzwang: he must move, and every move makes his position worse. In this example, on his turn his king or knight would have to give way. So hand him the move: bishop to g3. [%cal Ye5g3] }
1. Bg3 { The bishop steps back along the same diagonal. It still guards c7, and his knight cannot reach it on g3. [%cal Gg3c7] } (1. Bh2 { The bishop goes all the way back to h2. } 1... Ng5 { Also wins: from h2 the bishop still guards c7, and he still has to move. [%cal Gh2c7] }) (1. Bb8 { The bishop goes to b8, the other end of the diagonal. } 1... Ng5 { Also wins: from b8 the bishop still guards c7, and the waiting works just as well. [%cal Gb8c7] }) (1. Bd6+ { A check from d6, still on the same diagonal. [%cal Gd6c7] } 1... Kf6 { Also wins: his king had to step off e7, and your bishop still covers c7. [%cal Gd6c7] }) (1. c7 { The c pawn runs at once, into the knight's reach. } 1... Nxc7+ 2. Bxc7 Kf6 3. Ke4 Kg5 4. Kf3 Kxh6 { A draw: the knight gave itself for your c pawn, and his king ate your h pawn. A bishop alone cannot win. [%csl Rh6] }) (1. Bf6+ { A check from f6, but nothing guards the bishop there. } 1... Kxf6 { Lost: his king simply took the bishop, and your two pawns are no match for his king and knight. [%csl Rf6] }) (1. Bf4 { The bishop steps to f4, one of the squares his knight covers. } 1... Nxf4+ { Lost: the knight takes the bishop with check, and you are left with two pawns. [%csl Rf4] }) (1. Bc7 { The bishop goes to c7, in front of your own pawn. } 1... Nxc7+ { Lost: on c7 the bishop stood right in front of your own pawn, and his knight took it with check. [%csl Rc7] }) (1. Bd4 { The bishop steps to d4, another square in the knight's reach. } 1... Nxd4 2. Kxd4 Kd6 3. c7 Kxc7 { A draw: bishop for knight, then his king ate your c pawn. The two h pawns block each other and nobody can win. [%csl Rh6,Rh7] }) 1... Kf7 { His king steps to f7. Keep handing him the move: bishop to h2, still on the same diagonal. [%cal Yg3h2] } 2. Bh2 { The bishop waits on h2 and still guards c7. His king has nowhere useful to go. [%cal Gh2c7] } (2. Bb8 { The bishop goes the other way, to b8. } 2... Kf6 { Also wins: at the far end of the diagonal the bishop still guards c7 and waits just as well as on h2. [%cal Gb8c7] }) (2. Be5 { The bishop hurries straight back to e5. } 2... Ke7 { Also wins, but you are back where you began: his king returned to e7, and it is your move. [%csl Ge7] }) (2. Bd6 { The bishop steps to d6. } 2... Kf6 { Also wins: on d6 it still guards c7 and keeps waiting, and his king only shuffles. [%cal Gd6c7] }) (2. Ke5 { Your king steps across to e5. } 2... Nc7 { Also wins, but slowly: his knight now blocks your pawn from c7, and it takes many more moves to dislodge it. [%csl Rc7] }) (2. Kc4 { Your king steps back to c4. } 2... Kf6 { Also wins: a king step is just another way to wait, and your bishop still guards c7. [%cal Gg3c7] }) (2. Ke4 { Your king steps back to e4. } 2... Ng5+ { Also wins, though his knight gets a check in from g5 before you go back to waiting. [%csl Rg5] }) (2. Bh4 { The bishop hops to h4, off the diagonal. } 2... Nc7+ { Also wins, but slowly: off the diagonal the bishop let his knight jump to c7 with check and block your pawn. [%csl Rc7] }) (2. Be1 { The bishop retreats all the way to e1. } 2... Nc7+ { Also wins, but very slowly: from e1 your bishop no longer guards c7, and his knight settles there with check. [%csl Rc7] }) (2. Bf2 { The bishop slides back to f2. } 2... Nc7+ { Also wins, if slowly: f2 is off the diagonal as well, and his knight blocks your pawn from c7 with check. [%csl Rc7] }) (2. c7 { The c pawn runs now. } 2... Nxc7+ 3. Bxc7 Kg6 4. Ke5 Kxh6 { A draw: knight for pawn, then his king eats your last pawn on h6. A lone bishop is not enough. [%csl Rh6] }) (2. Kd6 { Your king steps forward to d6. } 2... Nd4 3. c7 Nb5+ 4. Kc5 Nxc7 5. Bxc7 { A draw: the knight slipped out with check and gave itself for your c pawn, and your h pawn is stuck behind his pawn on h7. [%csl Rh7] }) (2. Bf4 { The bishop steps onto f4. } 2... Nxf4+ { Lost: f4 belongs to his knight, which takes the bishop with check. [%csl Rf4] }) (2. Bc7 { The bishop goes to c7, right in front of your pawn. } 2... Nxc7+ { Lost: c7 is covered by his knight, which takes the bishop with check. [%csl Rc7] }) 2... Ke7 { His king comes back to e7. Now the bishop to e5: then it is his move, in the very position you started from. [%cal Yh2e5] } 3. Be5 { Back on e5, and now it is his move: you handed it over, just as the spare pawn step did. That is zugzwang. Watch. [%tail] } (3. Bg3 { The bishop steps to g3 and keeps waiting. } 3... Kf7 { Also wins, but it wastes the moment: his king steps to f7, and the circle starts from the beginning. [%csl Gf7] }) (3. Bd6+ { A check from d6. } 3... Kf6 { Also wins, but the check lets his king off e7, and the zugzwang is gone for now. [%csl Ge7] }) (3. Bb8 { The bishop steps to b8. } 3... Ng5 { Also wins, but slower: on e5 your bishop would have caught him in zugzwang at once. [%csl Ge5] }) (3. c7 { The c pawn goes now. } 3... Nxc7+ 4. Bxc7 Kf6 5. Ke4 Kg5 6. Kf3 Kxh6 { A draw: the pawn went one move too soon. The knight took it, and his king took your h pawn. [%csl Rh6] }) (3. Ke5 { Your king steps to e5. } 3... Ng5 4. c7 Nf7+ 5. Kd4 Kd7 6. c8=Q+ Kxc8 { A draw: with your king off d5, his knight checked it away and his king took the pawn on c8. [%csl Rc8] }) (3. Bf4 { The bishop stops on f4. } 3... Nxf4+ { Lost: f4 is one step short of e5, and his knight takes the bishop there with check. [%csl Rf4] }) (3. Bc7 { The bishop runs to c7. } 3... Nxc7+ { Lost: his knight takes it with check, and your pawn stays stuck. [%csl Rc7] }) 3... Ng5 4. Bf6+ { [%cal Gf6e7,Gc6c8] } 4... Kxf6 5. c7 Kf5 6. c8=Q+ { A new queen on c8, with check. Zugzwang made his knight leave the pawn's road, and your bishop gave itself up to drag his king away. [%csl Gc8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca and Lasker endgames: zugzwang, the knight cannot wait"]
