{ You have White and play to win. The bad bishop, from a master game played in Berlin, 1907: a bishop never leaves its colour, and four of his pawns sit on its light squares, hemming it in. [%csl Ra6,Rb5,Rd5,Rf5] }
1... Bh7 { His bishop steps to h7. He has nothing better. Push your h pawn to h4, onto a dark square like your other pawns. [%cal Yh3h4] } 2. h4 { Now h5 is threatened, which would take g6 from his bishop. If he stops it with his own pawn, that pawn stands on a light square too. } (2. Bb1 { The bishop steps back to b1. } 2... Bg6 { Also wins: b1 keeps the same diagonal, and the pawn push to h4 is still there. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (2. Kc3 { Your king steps to c3. } 2... Bg6 { Also wins: a king step that changes nothing, since his bishop stays just as stuck. [%csl Rg6] }) (2. Bc2 { The bishop steps to c2 first. } 2... Bg6 { Also wins: the bishop keeps its diagonal, and your h pawn can go to h4 next move. [%cal Gh3h4] }) (2. Bxb5 { Your bishop takes on b5! A sacrifice. } 2... axb5 3. a6 Kc7 4. Ke5 { Also wins, a different way: his king has to chase your a pawn, and yours walks in to eat his pawns. [%cal Ge5d5] }) (2. Be2 { The bishop heads for e2. } 2... Bg8 { Also wins: the bishop can go on to f3 and hit his d pawn, while your h pawn keeps his bishop off g4. [%csl Rd5] }) (2. Bf1 { The bishop goes all the way back to f1. } 2... Bg8 { Also wins: it takes the long way round toward f3, and his bishop still has no way out. [%csl Rg8] }) (2. e4 { Your e pawn breaks at d5. } 2... dxe4 3. Be2 Bg6 { A draw: the swap gave him a pawn on e4 and opened d5 for his bishop, which is free now. [%csl Gd5] }) (2. Bxf5 { Your bishop grabs the pawn on f5. } 2... Bxf5 { Lost: his bishop guarded that pawn, and you gave a whole bishop for it. [%csl Rf5] }) 2... Bg6 { His bishop comes back to g6 to guard his f pawn. Keep your bishop on its diagonal: to c2. [%cal Yd3c2] } 3. Bc2 { From c2 the bishop keeps pressing on f5, and his bishop stays shut in. Now watch. [%tail] } (3. Bb1 { The bishop steps to b1. } 3... Bh7 { Also wins: b1 is the far end of the same diagonal, and your bishop still eyes his f pawn. [%csl Rf5] }) (3. Kc3 { Your king steps back to c3. } 3... Ke6 { Also wins: a waiting king step, and his bishop is no better off for it. [%csl Rg6] }) (3. h5 { Your h pawn pushes on to h5. } 3... Bxh5 4. Bxf5 Be8 5. Bc8 { Also wins: pawns are level, but your bishop is behind his pawns now, and his king cannot guard them all. [%csl Ra6,Rb5] }) (3. Be2 { The bishop heads for e2 now. } 3... Bf7 { A draw: your bishop left its diagonal, and his got out to f7 at once, where it guards his d pawn. [%cal Gf7d5] }) (3. Bf1 { The bishop swings back to f1. } 3... Be8 4. Bh3 Bd7 { A draw: his bishop slipped out to d7 while yours was away, and none of his pawns is weak enough to win now. [%csl Gd7] }) (3. e4 { Your e pawn breaks now. } 3... dxe4 4. Be2 Be8 { A draw: the swap opened d5 for his bishop, and your chance to win a pawn is gone. [%csl Gd5] }) (3. Bxb5 { Your bishop gives itself up on b5. } 3... axb5 4. a6 Be8 { Lost: his bishop can stop your a pawn on a8, a light square, and you are a piece down. [%csl Ra8] }) (3. Bxf5 { The bishop takes the f pawn. } 3... Bxf5 { Lost: the pawn was guarded, and his bishop takes yours. You are a bishop down. [%csl Rf5] }) 3... h5 4. Bd3 Bh7 5. Bf1 Bg8 6. Be2 Bf7 7. Bf3 { [%cal Gf3d5,Gf3h5] } 7... Be6 8. Bxh5 { A pawn up, and his bishop, tied to d5, is still boxed in by his own pawns. That is the bad bishop. [%cal Re6d5][%csl Gh5] } 1-0


