{ You have White and you are a pawn down: hold the draw. His f pawn is two steps from queening, and his bishop can only ever touch dark squares. }
1. Nd2 { The knight jumps to d2 and watches f1, the square his pawn must cross. [%cal Gd2f1] } (1. Nd4 { The knight jumps to d4 and hits the pawn. } 1... Bxd4 2. Kxd4 f2 3. Kc4 f1=Q+ { Lost: his bishop took the knight, and the pawn queened with nothing left to stop it. [%csl Rf1] }) (1. Ke4 { Your king runs at the pawn. } 1... f2 2. Nd2 Kg2 3. Kd3 Ba5 4. Nb1 f1=Q+ { Lost: your king got there too late, his bishop chased the knight off d2, and the pawn queened with check. [%csl Rf1] }) (1. Kc3 { Your king steps off to c3, away from his f pawn. } 1... f2 2. Nd2 Ba5+ 3. Kb2 Bxd2 { Lost: his bishop checked from a5 and took your knight, and nothing stops the pawn now. [%csl Rf1] }) (1. Kd2 { Your king heads for e2, next to the queening square. } 1... f2 2. Ke2 Kg2 3. Nd2 Bc7 { Lost: king and knight guard f1 twice, but his bishop can reach f4 and swap itself for the knight, and then the pawn queens. [%cal Ge2f1,Gd2f1] }) 1... f2 { His pawn steps to f2, one step from queening. } 2. Ne4+ { Check from e4: his king must move, and your knight now hits the pawn on f2 too. [%cal Ge4f2] } (2. Nf1+ { Check from f1, right in front of the pawn. } 2... Kg2 3. Ke2 Bd4 4. Nd2 Be5 5. Nf1 Bf4 { Lost: your knight is stuck guarding f1, and every move you have now drops it or lets the pawn queen. [%csl Rf1] }) (2. Ke2 { Your king steps to e2 to guard the queening square. [%cal Ge2f1] } 2... Kg2 3. Nf1 Bd4 4. Nd2 Be5 5. Nf1 Bf4 { Lost: his bishop on f4 takes away your last good squares, and whatever you move, something falls. [%csl Rf1] }) (2. Kc3 { The king walks off to c3. } 2... Ba5+ 3. Kb2 Bxd2 { Lost: the check from a5 drove your king away, and his bishop won the knight that guarded f1. [%csl Rf1] }) (2. Ke4 { Your king steps toward the pawn. } 2... Kg2 3. Kd3 Ba5 4. Nb1 f1=Q+ { Lost: without the check from e4, his bishop drives the knight away and the pawn queens. [%csl Ge4] }) 2... Kg2 { His king steps to g2 to guard the pawn. } 3. Nxf2 { The knight gives itself up for the pawn. Now only his h pawn is left to win with. } (3. Ke2 { Your king steps to e2 to guard f1. [%cal Ge2f1] } 3... f1=Q+ { Lost: the pawn queens with check, and his king on g2 guards the new queen. [%csl Rf1] }) (3. Nd2 { The knight retreats to d2 to guard f1. [%cal Gd2f1] } 3... Ba5 4. Nb1 f1=Q+ { Lost: his bishop hit the knight from a5, and when it moved the pawn queened. [%csl Rf1] }) (3. Kd2 { Your king heads for e1. } 3... f1=Q { Lost: one step short, and the pawn queens on f1 at once. [%csl Rf1] }) (3. Ng3 { The knight jumps to g3. } 3... Kxg3 { Lost: the knight gave itself up without touching the pawn, and your king alone cannot stop it with his king beside it. [%csl Rf2] }) 3... Kxf2 { His king takes the knight. Now it is your king against his bishop and h pawn. } 4. Kd2 { Your king steps to d2. Any king move holds for now: his king still has to go and take your h pawn. } (4. Kc2 { Your king steps to c2. } 4... Kg3 { Also a draw, but c2 is a step further from the corner, and now only a king move to the d column keeps it. [%csl Gh1] }) (4. Kc3 { The king sidesteps to c3. } 4... Kg3 { Also a draw, but only if your king comes straight back, to d2 or d3. [%csl Gh1] }) (4. Kc4 { Your king walks to c4. } 4... Kg3 { Also a draw, but only just: now the one move that holds is back to d3. [%csl Gd3] }) (4. Ke4 { Your king steps forward to e4. } 4... Kg3 { Also a draw, if you find the one move left: back to d3, on the road to the corner. [%csl Gd3] }) 4... Kg3 { His king heads for your h pawn. } 5. Ke2 { Your king steps to e2, on its way to the corner. [%cal Ge2f1] } (5. Kd1 { Your king steps to d1. } 5... Kg4 { Also a draw: your king can still reach the corner along the first row. [%csl Gh1] }) (5. Ke1 { Your king steps to e1. } 5... Kg4 { Also a draw: e1 is just as close to the corner as e2. [%csl Gh1] }) (5. Kd3 { Your king steps forward to d3. } 5... Kg4 { Also a draw: from d3 the corner is one step further away, but your king still gets there in time. [%csl Gh1] }) (5. Kc3 { Your king turns toward his bishop. } 5... Kg4 6. Kb4 Kxh5 { Lost: his king took your h pawn, and your king on b4 is six moves from h1, too far to stop his pawn. [%csl Rh1] }) (5. Kc2 { The king drifts to c2. } 5... Kg4 6. Kb1 Kxh5 { Lost: his king has your h pawn, and his pawn has a free road while your king sits on b1. [%csl Rh1] }) (5. Kc1 { The king slips to c1. } 5... Kg4 6. Kb1 Kxh5 { Lost: the corner is out of your king's reach, and his king has taken your h pawn. [%csl Rh1] }) 5... Kg4 { His king steps closer to your h pawn. } 6. Kf1 { Your king steps to f1, next to the corner. [%cal Gf1g2] } (6. Ke1 { The king waits on e1. } 6... Kxh5 { Also a draw: from e1 your king still gets to g2 in time. [%csl Gg2] }) (6. Kd1 { Your king steps back to d1. } 6... Kxh5 7. Ke1 Kg4 8. Kf1 Kf3 { Lost: his king on f3 and his bishop's diagonal to g1 shut your king out of the corner. [%csl Rg2,Rg1] }) (6. Kd2 { Your king steps to d2. } 6... Kxh5 7. Ke1 Kg4 8. Kf1 Kf3 { Lost: you are one step too slow, and his king on f3 bars the way to g2. [%csl Rg2] }) (6. Kd3 { Your king returns to d3. } 6... Kxh5 7. Ke2 Kg4 8. Kf1 Kf3 { Lost: his king reached f3 first, and with his bishop watching g1 your king can never get to the corner. [%csl Rg1] }) 6... Kxh5 { His king takes your h pawn. } 7. Kg2 { Your king takes g2, and the corner is yours: his pawn can only queen on h1, a light square. Watch his try. [%csl Gh1][%tail] } (7. Ke1 { The king steps across to e1. } 7... Kg4 8. Kf1 Kf3 { Lost: one step late, and his king on f3 keeps yours out of the corner for good. [%csl Rf3] }) (7. Ke2 { Your king steps to e2. } 7... Kg4 8. Kf1 Kf3 { Lost: his king on f3 and his bishop covering g1 keep your king out of the corner. [%csl Rg1,Rg2] }) 7... Kg4 8. Kh2 h5 9. Kg2 h4 10. Kh2 h3 11. Kh1 { Draw. Your king sits on h1, the square his pawn needs, and his bishop runs on dark squares, so it can never drive you out. [%csl Gh1] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca and Lasker endgames: bishop against knight, the rook goes hunting"]
[Result "1-0"]
