{ Bishop against knight: with pawns on both wings the bishop is stronger, because it works from far away. Your bishop on c4 guards your a pawn. Capablanca, world champion from 1921, lost this ending and later explained why. [%cal Gc4a2] }
30... a6 { He pushes his a pawn, a slow move: his king to d6 was better. You are White and a pawn up: win. Check on f7 first. [%cal Yf5f7] } 31. Rf7+ { Check, and your rook reaches the seventh row, beside his g pawn. His rook would like your a pawn, but your bishop guards it. [%cal Gc4a2] } (31. Rg5 { The rook on g5 hits his g pawn, but the pawn can step out of the way. [%cal Rg7g6] } 31... g6 32. h4 Ne7 33. a4 { Better for you, but both his king side pawns are safe now. The check on f7 was the way in. [%cal Gf5f7] }) (31. h4 { A calm pawn move, and it gives his rook time to find a target. [%csl Rb2] } 31... Re2 32. Bd5 Kd6 33. Be4 { Better for you, but his rook is busy on e2 and your rook has not touched a pawn yet. [%csl Re2] }) (31. Rd5+ { A check from the d column, but his king can answer by stepping to e7, next to his king side pawns. [%csl Re7] } 31... Ke7 32. Rf5 b5 33. Bb3 { Better for you, but your rook is back where it started, and no pawn has fallen. [%cal Gf5f7] }) (31. Bb3 { The bishop steps back to b3 to guard the a pawn, which it already did from c4. [%csl Ga2] } 31... Re2 32. Bd5 Kd6 33. Be4 { Better for you, but a whole move went on guarding what was already safe, and his rook got to e2. [%csl Re2] }) (31. Bxa6 { The bishop grabs a6, but his b pawn can take it back. [%cal Rb7a6] } 31... bxa6 32. Rf7+ Ke6 { Level: you gave your bishop for one pawn, and your whole advantage went with it. [%csl Ra6] }) 31... Kd6 { His king steps to d6. Take the g pawn. [%cal Yf7g7] } 32. Rxg7 { One pawn won, and the rook now hits h7. Your bishop on c4 keeps his rook off the a pawn from across the board. [%cal Gc4a2,Gg7h7] } (32. Bb3 { The bishop steps to b3 before taking anything, and that gives his knight time. [%csl Rc6] } 32... Ne5 33. Rxg7 Nxd3 { Much better for you still, but his knight ate d3: one pawn won, one pawn lost. [%csl Rd3] }) (32. Rxb7 { The rook grabs b7, but his rook on b2 guards that square along the column. [%cal Rb2b7] } 32... Rxb7 { Lost for you: a whole rook for one pawn. [%csl Rb7] }) (32. Rf6+ { A check, but the rook stands where his g pawn can take it. [%cal Rg7f6] } 32... gxf6 { Lost for you: the pawn took your rook, and you have nothing for it. }) 32... b5 { His pawn kicks your bishop. Where can it go and keep guarding the a pawn? Slide it all the way to g8. [%cal Yc4g8,Rb5c4] } 33. Bg8 { Along the same diagonal the bishop still guards the a pawn, and from g8 it hits h7 as well: one piece at work on both sides of the board. [%cal Gg8a2,Gg8h7] } (33. Bb3 { Also strong: the bishop keeps guarding the a pawn from b3, though it does not hit h7 from there. [%csl Ga2] } 33... a5 34. Rb7 Nd4 { Much better for you as well. From g8 your bishop would also have hit his h pawn. [%cal Gc4g8] }) (33. Bf7 { The bishop stays on the diagonal and still guards the a pawn, but on f7 it hits nothing. [%csl Gf7] } 33... Ne5 34. Bg8 Nxd3 { Better for you, but his knight chased your bishop and then took d3. [%csl Rd3] }) (33. Bxb5 { The bishop grabs the b pawn, but his a pawn is there to recapture. [%cal Ra6b5] } 33... axb5 34. Rxh7 Rxa2 { Much better for him: your bishop is gone, and his rook ate your a pawn. [%csl Ra2] }) (33. Bd5 { The bishop steps into the middle, right next to his king. [%csl Rd5] } 33... Kxd5 { Much better for him: his king simply took your bishop. }) (33. Be6 { The bishop lands on e6, one step from his king. [%csl Re6] } 33... Kxe6 { Much better for him: the king collected your bishop for nothing. }) (33. Rxh7 { The rook takes h7 and leaves your bishop to his pawn. [%csl Rc4] } 33... bxc4 34. dxc4 { Level: a bishop for two pawns, and your whole edge is gone. [%csl Rc4] }) 33... Ne7 { His knight chases your bishop from e7. Keep it on the same diagonal: back to b3. [%cal Yg8b3,Re7g8] } 34. Bb3 { From b3 the bishop still guards your a pawn, so the rook is free to take h7. Watch. [%tail][%cal Gb3a2,Gg7h7] } (34. Bxh7 { Just as good: the bishop takes h7 itself, but it lets go of the a pawn. [%csl Ra2] } 34... Rxa2 35. h4 { Much better for you too: a pawn each way, and you are still two pawns up. [%csl Gh4] }) (34. Bf7 { Also good: from f7 the bishop keeps its watch over the a pawn. [%cal Gf7a2] } 34... Nf5 35. Rxh7 { Much better for you as well: the rook takes h7 all the same, and you are three pawns up. [%csl Gh7] }) (34. Rxh7 { The rook takes h7, but your bishop on g8 is still hanging to his knight. [%csl Rg8] } 34... Nxg8 { Better for him: your bishop went for one pawn. [%csl Rg8] }) (34. Bc4 { Back to c4, where it came from, but his b pawn still attacks that square. [%cal Rb5c4] } 34... bxc4 35. dxc4 { Level: the pawn took your bishop, and one pawn back does not pay for it. [%csl Rc4] }) (34. Bd5 { The bishop steps to d5, where his knight on e7 covers it. [%cal Re7d5] } 34... Nxd5 { Lost for you: the knight took your bishop for nothing. }) (34. Be6 { The bishop lands right next to his king on d6. [%csl Re6] } 34... Kxe6 { Much better for him: his king swallowed your bishop. }) 34... a5 35. Rxh7 { The rook takes h7 too: three pawns up, and your bishop still guards the a pawn from b3. That is bishop against knight. [%csl Gb3] } 1-0


