{ You have Black and you are two pawns down: hold the draw. Bishops of opposite colours help the defender. }
1. c4+ { He breaks with the c pawn, with check: his best try to get a free pawn on the queen side. } 1... Kxd4 { Your king takes the d pawn. Now his c pawn faces your b pawn, and your king is close by. } (1... bxc4 { Your b pawn takes instead. } 2. Bc3 Ke6 { Also a draw: your c pawn keeps his bishop busy, while your king and bishop watch his two pawns. [%csl Gc4] }) (1... Kxc4 { Your king takes the c pawn. } 2. Kf4 Kxd4 { Also a draw: your king eats both his centre pawns, and a pawn each is left with bishops of opposite colours. [%csl Gd4] }) (1... Kc6 { Your king steps back to c6. } 2. d5+ Kc5 3. Kf4 bxc4 { Lost: the check freed his d pawn, and his two free pawns, far apart, are too much for your king and bishop. [%csl Rd5,Rg5] }) (1... Kd6 { Your king steps back to d6. } 2. cxb5 { Lost: he takes your b pawn, and now he has three pawns against none. [%csl Rb5] }) (1... Ke6 { Your king retreats to e6. } 2. cxb5 { Lost: your b pawn falls for free, and his three pawns are too many for your bishop. [%csl Rb5] }) (1... Ke4 { Your king steps toward his g pawn. } 2. cxb5 { Lost: the king went the wrong way, and his pawns now outnumber yours three to none. [%csl Rb5] }) 2. cxb5 { He takes your b pawn, and his new b pawn is free. } 2... Kc5 { Your king steps to c5, next to his b pawn. } (2... Kd5 { Your king steps to d5. } 3. b6 Kc6 4. Ba5 { Lost: his bishop on a5 now guards the pawn, and your king can never take it. [%csl Rb6] }) (2... Kc4 { Your king steps to c4. } 3. b6 Be4 4. g6 { Lost: he has two free pawns far apart, and your bishop cannot stop both. [%csl Rb6,Rg6] }) (2... Ke5 { Your king goes after his g pawn. } 3. b6 Kd6 4. Ba5 { Lost: while your king went the other way, his b pawn got too far ahead, and his bishop guards it. [%csl Rb6] }) (2... Kd3 { Your king goes after his bishop. } 3. Kf4 Kxd2 4. b6 { Lost: you won his bishop, but his two free pawns, far apart, are too many for your bishop alone. [%csl Rb6,Rg5] }) (2... Bf5+ { Check with your bishop on f5. } 3. Kxf5 { Lost: f5 is next to his king, which simply takes the bishop. [%csl Rf5] }) (2... Bh5+ { Check with your bishop from h5. } 3. Kxh5 { Lost: his king takes the bishop, and you have nothing left to stop his pawns. [%csl Rh5] }) (2... Be4 { Your bishop steps to e4, covering b7 and the square in front of his g pawn. [%cal Ge4b7,Ge4g6] } 3. b6 Kc5 4. Be3+ Kc6 5. Kh5 { This loses: his bishop guards the pawn on b6, so your king cannot take it, and his king heads for h6 to escort the g pawn past your bishop. [%cal Re3b6,Rh5h6] }) (2... Be8 { Your bishop swings to e8 to hit the b pawn. } 3. b6 { Lost: the pawn steps to b6, out of your bishop's reach, and your king on d4 is one step too far to take it in time. [%csl Rb6] }) (2... Bd3 { Your bishop goes to d3 to hit the b pawn. } 3. b6 { Lost: the pawn simply runs out of your bishop's reach, and your king is too far to help. [%csl Rb6] }) 3. b6 { His pawn runs on to b6, out of your bishop's reach. } 3... Kxb6 { Your king takes it. His last pawn, on g5, is blocked by your bishop on g6, which his dark bishop can never chase away. Watch his try. [%tail] } (3... Be4 { Your bishop goes to e4 to watch b7, and the pawn stays on the board. } 4. Be3+ Kc6 5. Kh5 Bf3+ 6. Kh6 { This loses: the b pawn was yours to take, and now his bishop guards it. His king has reached h6 to walk the g pawn through, and your bishop stands alone against it. [%cal Re3b6][%csl Rg5] }) (3... Kc6 { Your king steps to c6. } 4. Ba5 { Lost: with his bishop on a5 guarding it, the pawn cannot be taken, and it runs on. [%csl Rb6] }) (3... Kd6 { Your king steps to d6. } 4. Ba5 Kc6 5. Kf4 { Lost: his bishop holds the b pawn while his king walks over to help it home. [%csl Rb6] }) (3... Kb5 { Your king steps to b5. } 4. b7 Bd3 5. b8=Q+ { Lost: the pawn slipped past your king to b8, a dark square, and queened with check. [%csl Rb8] }) (3... Kd5 { Your king heads for d5 instead. } 4. b7 Ke4 5. b8=Q { Lost: your king stood one step too far, and the pawn queened on b8. [%csl Rb8] }) (3... Bf5+ { A check from f5. } 4. Kxf5 { Lost: his king takes the bishop on f5, and the b pawn runs free. [%csl Rf5] }) (3... Bh5+ { A check from h5. } 4. Kxh5 { Lost: his king took your bishop on h5, and nothing stops his pawns now. [%csl Rh5] }) 4. Kf4 Kc6 5. Ke5 Kd7 6. Kf6 Bh7 { Draw: his king chased your bishop, and it stepped to h7, still guarding the square in front of his pawn. His dark bishop can never touch that square. [%csl Gh7] } 0-1


