{ The second weakness: he can defend one weak pawn, but not two far apart. Both his rooks are tied to the pawn on a3, and his king side is the second target. Capablanca, world champion from 1921, had Black here. [%csl Ra3,Rg2][%cal Ra4a3] }
7. Kc2 { His king heads for the queen side, to guard the a pawn and free his rooks; a pawn move on the king side was safer. You are Black, level in material: win. Swing a rook to g5. [%cal Ya5g5] } 7... Rg5 { The rook swings across and hits g2, which nothing guards: that is the second target, while his rooks are still busy with the pawn on a3. [%cal Gg5g2][%csl Ra3] } (7... Kf6 { Your king heads for the middle, but it gives his king time to reach the queen side. [%csl Rb3] } 8. Kb3 Rf4 9. Rc2 Rb5+ 10. Ka2 { Slightly better for you: his king guards the a pawn now, and his rooks are free to move. [%csl Ra3] }) (7... Rb5 { The rook covers b3 to keep his king out, still on the queen side. [%csl Gb3] } 8. Rd1 Rg5 9. Kb3 Re4 { Better for you, but his king got to b3 all the same. The rook switch to g5 was a move faster. [%cal Ga5g5] }) (7... Rc5 { The rook hits c3, his other weak pawn, on the side where his pieces already stand. [%csl Rc3] } 8. Kb3 Re4 9. a4 Rd5 { About level: his king reached b3, and both his weak pawns are covered. [%csl Rb3] }) (7... Rf5 { Your rook slides to f5 to hit his f pawn, but the pawn can just step up. [%csl Rf2] } 8. Kb3 Raf4 9. f3 Rb5+ 10. Kc2 { About level: the pawn on f3 shut the door, and his king is safe on the queen side. [%csl Rf3] }) (7... Re5 { The rook takes the e column, but there is nothing to hit on it. [%csl Re2] } 8. Kb3 Rae4 9. a4 Re2 10. Rxe2 Rxe2 { Slightly better for you: a pair of rooks came off, and with them your chance to hit two things at once. [%csl Ge2] }) 8. Rg1 { He pulls a rook back to guard g2, and only one rook is left on the a pawn. Bring your other rook across as well. [%cal Ya4f4,Rg1g2] } 8... Rf4 { Both your rooks now aim at his king side: this one hits f2, and his rook on g1 cannot guard it. [%cal Gf4f2][%csl Rf2] } (8... Kf6 { Your king joins in slowly, and his f pawn gets time to step up to f3. [%csl Rf2] } 9. f3 Raa5 10. Kd3 Rgd5+ 11. Kc4 { Better for you, but the pawn on f3 shut the door, and your rooks went back to the queen side. [%csl Rf3] }) (8... Rc4 { The rook goes to c4 to hit his pawn on c3, but his king is close enough to guard it. [%csl Rc3] } 9. Kd3 Rf4 { Better for you, but you lost a move: the rook came to f4 one move later than it could have. [%cal Ga4f4] }) (8... Re4 { The rook moves to the e column, where it hits nothing. [%csl Re4] } 9. Kd3 Rf4 { Better for you, though his king used the extra move to come back to d3. [%csl Rd3] }) (8... Rf5 { The rook leaves the g column to hit the f pawn, and his pawn simply steps forward. [%csl Rf2] } 9. f3 Rc5 10. Kb3 Rac4 { Slightly better for you: the pawn on f3 is safe, his king reached b3, and your rooks are back on the queen side. [%csl Rb3] }) 9. Kd3 { His king hurries back to help. Check on f3: if his pawn takes, your other rook takes his rook on g1. [%cal Yf4f3] } 9... Rf3+ { Check! If his g pawn takes, your rook on g5 takes his rook, and his pawn on h3 loses its only guard. [%cal Gg5g1][%csl Rh3] } (9... Rd5+ { A check from d5, but his king can step up to e3 and guard the king side. [%csl Re3] } 10. Ke3 Rf6 11. f3 Re6+ 12. Kf2 { Better for you, but his king got back to f2 and the king side pawns are safe. [%csl Rf2] }) (9... Rxf2 { The rook grabs f2, but his rook on a2 guards it along the second row. [%cal Ra2f2] } 10. Rxf2 { Lost for you: a rook for one pawn. [%csl Rf2] }) (9... Kf6 { A quiet king move, and he gets a free move of his own. [%csl Rd3] } 10. a4 Rf3+ 11. gxf3 Rxg1 { Better for you: the check on f3 still worked a move later, but the pawn to a4 was a free move for him. [%csl Ra4] }) 10. gxf3 { He takes, his best try. Now take his rook on g1. } 10... Rxg1 { Rook for rook, and his pawn on h3 has no guard left. Watch your rook go and collect it. [%tail][%csl Rh3] } 11. a4 Rh1 12. Ke4 Rxh3 { The h pawn falls: you are a pawn up, and your h pawn is passed. His rooks were tied to a3 while yours switched wings: the second weakness. [%csl Gh4] } 0-1


