{ Havana, 1913: you are Capablanca, world champion from 1921, with White and a pawn up in a rook ending: win. One of his pawns is already weak; find a second. }
41... Re4 { His rook goes after your c pawn. A mistake: his king to e6, hitting your rook, was better. [%csl Rc4] } 42. Rxd3 { Yes: the rook takes the d pawn that his rook just left. You are two pawns up for the moment. [%csl Gd3] } (42. a3 { The pawn guards b4, but his passed d pawn stays on the board. [%cal Ga3b4][%csl Rd3] } 42... Rxc4 43. Rxd3 Ke6 44. Rh3 { Better for you, but a move slower: his king came to e6 while you were busy. [%csl Re6] }) (42. c5 { The c pawn runs from his rook, but your b pawn is left behind. [%csl Rb4] } 42... Rxb4 43. Rxd3 Rb2+ 44. Kf3 { About level: you won d3, but his rook took b4 and now sits on your second row. [%csl Rb2] }) (42. Kf3 { Your king attacks his rook, but the rook is free to take your c pawn and then the b pawn. [%csl Rb4] } 42... Rxc4 43. Rxd3 Rxb4 44. Ra3 { Level: you won one pawn and he won two, so the pawns are even. [%csl Rb4] }) (42. b5 { The b pawn runs forward, but your c pawn is left to his rook, and his d pawn is still standing. [%csl Rd3] } 42... Rxc4 43. Rxd3 Rc2+ 44. Kf3 { Level: d3 fell, but his rook checked its way onto your second row, and a2 is loose. [%csl Ra2] }) (42. g3 { The g pawn hits his f pawn, but his rook is free to eat your c pawn first. [%cal Gg3f4] } 42... Rxc4 43. a3 Rc3 44. gxf4 { Level: you won his f pawn, but his d pawn is still alive and your a pawn hangs. [%csl Ra3] }) (42. Rf5 { The rook turns to his f pawn and leaves the d pawn alone for a move. [%csl Rd3] } 42... Rxc4 43. Rd5 Rxb4 44. Rxd3 { Level: your rook went to f5 and back for the d pawn, and his rook ate your b and c pawns. [%csl Rb4] }) 42... Rxc4 { He takes your c pawn, and his rook now hits your b pawn too. What now? [%csl Rb4] } 43. Rh3 { Found it: the rook swings to h3 and hits his h pawn, the second weak pawn, far from his rook. Let the b pawn go. [%tail][%cal Gh3h7] } (43. a3 { Pawn to a3 protects the b pawn, but it spends the move your rook needed. [%cal Ga3b4] } 43... Ke6 44. Rh3 Kf5 45. Rh5+ { Better for you, but his king came to f5 in time to cover the king side. [%csl Rf5] }) (43. Rb3 { The rook guards b4, and that ties it to the queen side. [%cal Gb3b4] } 43... Rc2+ 44. Kf3 Rxa2 45. Kxf4 { Level: his rook took your a pawn, and it now hits g2 as well. [%cal Ra2g2] }) (43. Kf3 { Your king goes for the f pawn, and your b pawn is left to his rook. [%csl Rb4] } 43... Rxb4 44. Ra3 Kf6 45. Rxa7 h5 { Level: you took a7 and he took b4, and his king and h pawn hold the king side. [%csl Rh5] }) (43. Ra3 { The rook turns to a7, a weak pawn on the same wing, and lets b4 go. [%csl Ra7] } 43... Rxb4 44. Ra6 Rb1 45. Rxa7+ Kf6 { Level: pawn for pawn, a7 for your b pawn, and nothing is gained. [%csl Rb1] }) (43. b5 { The b pawn runs from his rook, but your a pawn is left with no guard. [%csl Ra2] } 43... Rc2+ 44. Kf3 Rxa2 45. Rd4 { Level: the b pawn ran, but his rook got to your second row and took the a pawn. [%csl Ra2] }) (43. Rf3 { The rook hits his f pawn, but his rook guards it along the row, and your a pawn is loose. [%cal Rc4f4] } 43... Rc2+ 44. Kg1 Rxa2 45. Rxf4 { Level: you took his f pawn, but his rook took a2 and now sits on your second row. [%csl Ra2] }) (43. g3 { The g pawn hits his f pawn, but his rook can check first and then go after your a pawn. [%cal Gg3f4] } 43... Rc2+ 44. Kf3 fxg3 45. Kxg3 Rxa2 { Level: his f pawn is gone, but so is your a pawn, and the pawns are even. [%csl Ra2] }) 43... Rxb4 44. Rxh7+ Kf6 45. Rxa7 { Your rook took h7 and a7 while his took one pawn: three against one now. That is the second weakness: he could not guard both wings. [%csl Gh7] } 1-0
